{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Tamar your daughter-in-law has played the harlot.</b> For she was keeping herself for Shelah to perform levirate marriage, and it was their practice to hold them as a married woman until the near kinsman should come, like the matter of Ruth and the redeemer." "<b>And also, here she is with child.</b> She cannot hide it." "<b>And Judah said.</b> And was there no one in the generation greater than he to judge her? After all, Yitzhak and Yaakov were still alive. Rather, they all sat in judgment over her. And why did Judah open? Because in capital cases they begin from the side." "<b>Bring her out and let her be burned.</b> Rashi: she was the daughter of Shem, who was a priest; therefore they judged her with burning. Not because the law is so, for she was neither betrothed nor married, but only designated for the levir. And even after the Torah was given, she would have had only a negative prohibition; but they made a fence concerning forbidden relations, and most death penalties among the children of Noach are by the sword. And because she had an extra distinction — that she was a daughter of a priest — they judged her with burning, even though the priesthood was taken from Shem and given to Avraham, because he preceded the blessing of Avraham to the blessing of HaShem; even so, it was taken from his seed and not from him. And one may say: even if she were not the daughter of Shem, her judgment would still have been burning, for they supposed that the one who lay with Tamar was a Canaanite, and a Canaanite who lies with a daughter of Israel — she is drawn after him into the court of Shem; they decreed, as it is written: ״ויאמר יהודה הוציאוה ותשרף״. And even though Shem was not alive, nevertheless his court is called by his name, because they sat in judgment on his decree. And if you ask: Judah too is liable to burning, for she was his daughter-in-law, and one who lies with his daughter-in-law is punished by burning — one may answer: it was the custom in their generation that the elder performs levirate marriage; if the son is a minor, the father performs levirate marriage, and she intended to be married for the sake of levirate marriage."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {3 "The second interpretation's logic is compressed and elliptical in the original; rendered as literally as the syntax allows. The phrase דמשכה אבתריה (Aramaic: 'she is drawn after him') is rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:00.057875417Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}